Portfolio objectives and constraints specify return goals, risk, liquidity, horizon, taxes, and permitted assets.|Tuition due next year requires a different plan from retirement decades away.|Same desired return implies identical portfolios?|No|Yes|Only for young investors
An IPS documents objectives, constraints, allocation rules, responsibilities, and review conditions.|A written plan limits illiquid exposure before markets become stressful.|Its purpose includes…?|Consistent decision rules|Guaranteed returns|Permanent fixed prices
Risk tolerance is willingness; capacity is ability to bear loss; horizon affects recovery and cash needs.|An investor wants risk but needs funds next month.|Willingness alone determines appropriate risk?|No|Yes|Only with high income
Diversification combines imperfectly correlated exposures but cannot remove all common risk.|Two differently exposed assets can reduce portfolio variance.|More holdings automatically guarantee better diversification?|No|Yes|Only above 20 holdings
MPT links portfolio expected return and covariance-based risk under model assumptions.|Portfolio variance includes both individual variances and covariance terms.|Ignore covariance when combining assets?|No|Yes|Only at equal weights
The efficient frontier offers maximum expected return for each modelled risk level; minimum variance minimises estimated variance.|An inefficient portfolio has a better expected-return/risk alternative under the inputs.|Frontier estimates guarantee realised efficiency?|No|Yes|Only with many assets
CAL combines a risk-free asset with a risky portfolio; CML uses the model's market portfolio.|Different risky portfolios create different capital-allocation slopes.|Every CAL is the CML?|No|Yes|Only for leveraged portfolios
CAPM uses market beta; APT and factor models allow multiple systematic exposures under distinct assumptions.|A portfolio responds to market, size, and other estimated factors.|A factor model proves every exposure earns a stable premium?|No|Yes|Only historically
Strategic allocation sets long-term targets; tactical allocation makes shorter-term deviations.|Long-term equity target 60% temporarily becomes 50%.|That temporary deviation is…?|Tactical allocation|Only rebalancing|A change in accounting policy
Passive management tracks a defined market exposure; active management seeks different outcomes through choices.|An index fund targets its benchmark while an active fund deviates.|Passive investing removes market risk?|No|Yes|Only for broad indices
Tracking error measures active-return variability; active share measures holdings differences using a defined method.|A portfolio differs from benchmark holdings but retains similar factor exposures.|High active share guarantees high alpha?|No|Yes|Only for concentrated funds
Construction and sizing distribute risk across positions, constraints, and conviction.|One security is 50% of capital and dominates downside exposure.|Number of holdings alone measures concentration adequately?|No|Yes|Only for equities
Rebalancing restores intended allocation after drift or changed goals.|Equities rise from 60% to 70%; a rebalance restores the target.|Rebalancing guarantees superior returns?|No|Yes|Only annually
Benchmarks should match mandate, investable universe, and relevant exposures.|A domestic bond mandate is compared with a global equity index.|This benchmark is…?|Poorly matched|Automatically fair|Ideal if returns are high
Sharpe uses total volatility; Sortino downside deviation; Treynor beta; information ratio active risk.|10% return − 4% risk-free, over 12% volatility gives Sharpe 0.5.|Sharpe here is…?|0.5|0.83|1.5
Alpha is model-relative excess performance; attribution decomposes sources such as allocation and selection.|An equity-heavy portfolio wins because equities outperform bonds.|This necessarily proves stock-selection skill?|No|Yes|Only above benchmark
Drawdown measures decline from a prior peak; protection strategies carry costs and limitations.|₹100 falls to ₹70, a 30% drawdown.|Recovery to ₹100 requires approximately…?|42.9% gain|30% gain|70% gain
Liability-driven investing matches obligations; goals-based investing organises assets around dated needs.|Pension assets hedge interest sensitivity of promised benefits.|Success depends only on asset return?|No|Yes|Only for retirees
Institutional portfolios reflect spending, liabilities, governance, regulation, and liquidity constraints.|An endowment funds annual spending while holding some illiquid assets.|Illiquid allocations should consider…?|Spending and stress liquidity|Only headline returns|Only fund prestige
